There were two bugs in the Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Missing exported symbol ([38;5;10mlib.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner loads [38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m and looks up [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. The Rust library never exported this symbol — [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m was only an internal Rust function. I added a [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(...)[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that matches the C function signature the test harness expects.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Bytes vs bits mismatch in [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m ([38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is the subtle one. The C [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m function takes its [38;5;10mdatalen[0m parameter in [1mbits[22m (it does [38;5;10mleft = S->buflen >> 3[0m to get bytes, checks [38;5;10mdatalen >= 512[0m for 64-byte blocks, etc.). The standalone [38;5;10mblake256()[0m function correctly converts: [38;5;10mblake256_update(&S, in, inlen*8)[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
However, the C [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m passes [1mbyte counts[22m directly to [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);   // SPX_N = 16 (bytes, not bits)
blakeX_update(&S, optrand, SPX_N);
